    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d22">

                <pb facs="0140" n="14" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg14"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A12/73: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-10-26T17:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 26,
                                1925—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 5:34 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>183. Department’s circular telegram, October 22, 4 p.m.<anchor n="22"
                        xml:id="d22-f1"/> President’s remarks have received exceedingly little press
                    comment here; only article of any interest is in <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Vossische Zeitung</hi> of October 23.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <pb facs="0141" n="15" xml:id="pg15"/>
                <p>I believe, however, that German opinion in general for obvious reasons would
                    welcome call from President Coolidge for disarmament conference at
                    Washington.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
